{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794994456,"nonce":"55gCsqeje3TRXaMCDRZJW3fb8L4=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgUrnaA","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/210","origin":"github"},"title":"lu(backend=\"pardiso\"): a solve that is not compiled, after a compiled one, returns wrong values or aborts the process","message":"After a compiled (`jax.jit`) solve with `jno.solve.lu(backend=\"pardiso\")`, a solve of the same problem that is not compiled returns a wrong field without any error, or the process aborts with a glibc heap message. Each of the two solves alone is correct, and `backend=\"host\"` is correct in the same sequence.\n\nA workflow that compiles its loop and then checks or post-processes outside the compiled function (a finite-difference check of a gradient, a forward-mode derivative, one more solve for a plot) hits this.\n\nReproduced on release 0.4.2 and on `main` (b3dcdd22), 6 of 6 runs.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nCompliance of a 3-D cantilever with one density per cell (P1 tetrahedra, 1,431 unknowns). `python repro.py pardiso` is correct; `python repro.py pardiso compiled-first` is not.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nimport jax\njax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport jno\n\ninner, symgrad, trace = jno.np.inner, jno.np.symgrad, jno.np.trace\nbackend = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) \u003e 1 else \"pardiso\"\n\nd = jno.shape.box(0, 0, 0, 4, 1, 1, size=0.25).domain()\nd.tag(\"clamp\", lambda x, y, z: x \u003c 1e-9)\n_, s = d.fem_symbols(space=\"P0\", names=(\"r\", \"s\"))\nrho = jno.np.parameter(s, name=\"rho\")  # one density per cell\nE = 1e-9 + rho**3 * (1 - 1e-9)\nlam, mu = E * 0.3 / (1.3 * 0.4), E / 2.6\nu, phi = d.fem_symbols(value_shape=(3,), order=1)\nx, y, z = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)[:3]\neps = lambda w: symgrad(w, [x, y, z])\nf = jnp.array([0.0, 0.0, -1.0])\nfem = jno.fem([\n    lam * trace(eps(u)) * trace(eps(phi)) + 2 * mu * inner(eps(u), eps(phi), n_contract=2) - inner(f, phi.bind(x=x, y=y, z=z), n_contract=1),\n    u(*d.variable(\"clamp\", split=True)[:3]) - (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n])\nwork = inner(f, u.bind(x=x, y=y, z=z), n_contract=1)\nlu = jno.solve.lu(backend=backend)\ncompliance = lambda r: fem.eval(work, fem.solve(linear=lu, rho=r), args={\"rho\": r})\n\ncells = max(len(c) for c in d.mesh.cells_dict.values())\nr = jnp.full(cells, 0.5)\nw = jnp.cos(jnp.arange(cells, dtype=jnp.float64))\nprint(\"unknowns\", fem.dofs, flush=True)\nif \"compiled-first\" in sys.argv:\n    compiled = jax.jit(compliance)\n    for i in range(3):\n        print(f\"compiled, density scaled by 1.001^{i}: C = {float(compiled(r * 1.001**i)):.6f}\", flush=True)\nprint(f\"not compiled: C = {float(compliance(r)):.6f}\", flush=True)\nprint(f\"not compiled, forward mode: dC.w = {float(jax.jvp(compliance, (r,), (w,))[1]):.6f}\", flush=True)\n```\n\nCorrect values: `C = 4497.745138`, `dC.w = 450.189530` (PARDISO alone, the host backend, and a finite difference agree).\n\n| Run | Not compiled `C` | Not compiled `dC.w` | Exit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `pardiso` (nothing compiled before) | 4497.745138 | 450.189530 | 0 |\n| `host compiled-first` | 4497.745138 | 450.189530 | 0 |\n| `pardiso compiled-first`, run 1 | 3.401585 | | abort: `corrupted size vs. prev_size` |\n| `pardiso compiled-first`, run 2 | 3.397604 | 2.177890 | 0 |\n| `pardiso compiled-first`, run 3 | 3.448348 | 0.180628 | 0 |\n| `pardiso compiled-first`, run 4 | 3.134452 | -376.210801 | 0 |\n\nThe three compiled calls print the right values in every run (4497.745138, 4484.278844, 4470.852868). The wrong values change from run to run, and jNO's residual check on the factorisation does not fire. Other abort messages seen from the same sequence: `munmap_chunk(): invalid pointer`, `malloc(): unsorted double linked list corrupted`, `free(): invalid next size (normal)`, `malloc(): unaligned tcache chunk detected`, and a plain segmentation fault.\n\n## What narrows it\n\n- Order matters: not compiled first and compiled afterwards was not seen to fail; several compiled programs in a row are fine; a reverse-mode gradient not compiled, followed by more solves not compiled, is fine.\n- No gradient is needed: a compiled forward solve followed by a forward solve that is not compiled is enough.\n- Size matters: the same sequence passed on the same beam with 285 and 768 unknowns and fails with 1,431.\n- The values handed to the solver are finite (the non-finite guard in `_pardiso_host_solve` does not fire).\n\nThe varying wrong values and the glibc messages point to memory being written or read after it was released, somewhere between the factorisation cache (`_PARDISO_CACHE`) and the two ways the callback is entered. That is a reading of the symptoms, not a located cause.","files":null}]}